/**
 * @file CoreServices.dox
 * @author Alexandru ALEXANDRESCU
 * @brief Doxygen documentation for the Core services: EventManager, ServiceBaseQueue, ServiceManager
 * 
 * @page ServicesPage Core Services
 *
 * @brief Event-driven service architecture with thread-isolated service base and centralized manager
 *
 * ## Overview
 * This module provides the building blocks for an event-driven, multi-threaded service architecture:
 * - @ref EventManagerPage EventManager centralized event dispatching.
 * - @ref ServiceBaseQueuePage ServiceBaseQueue thread-isolated service base with internal mailbox.
 * - @ref ServiceManagerPage ServiceManager owns, starts, and stops services safely.
 * - @ref ServicesTemplatesPage "Services Templates" for a quick start.
 *
 * ## EventManager
 * @brief Central event dispatcher.
 *
 * - Handles subscription of services to specific events.
 * - Dispatches events to subscribed services in a thread-safe manner.
 * - Can be used with multiple services that communicate via events.
 *
 * ### Usage
 * ```cpp
 * auto eventManager = std::make_shared<Core::EventManager>();
 * // services subscribe to events
 * serviceA->subscribe(Core::EventType::TestEvent);
 * eventManager->postEvent(Core::Event{Core::EventType::TestEvent, Core::ServiceId::TestService});
 * ```
 *
 * ### Notes
 * - Must outlive all services.
 * - Thread-safe dispatching.
 * - Can use a maximum number of event types and subscribers for static arrays.
 *
 * ## ServiceBaseQueue
 * @brief Base class for services with isolated event queue.
 *
 * - Each service runs in its own thread.
 * - Receives events from EventManager asynchronously.
 * - Provides `dispatchLoop()` and `dispatchLoopBlocking()` for non-blocking or batch processing.
 *
 * ### Usage
 * ```cpp
 * class MyService : public Core::ServiceBaseQueue {
 * public:
 *     void dispatch(const Core::Event& e) override {
 *         // process event
 *     }
 *
 *     void update() override {
 *         // periodic tasks
 *     }
 * };
 *
 * auto myService = std::make_shared<MyService>(Core::ServiceId::TestService, eventManager);
 * myService->init();
 * myService->start();
 * std::thread t([myService]{ myService->run(); });
 * ```
 *
 * ### Notes
 * - `start()` must be called before the service thread runs.
 * - `stop()` signals termination.
 * - `onEvent()` is non-blocking.
 * - Event queue size and maximum events per cycle can be configured.
 *
 * ## ServiceManager
 * @brief Manages service lifetimes and threads.
 *
 * - Owns all services and their threads.
 * - Responsible for startup and shutdown order:
 *   1. EventManager
 *   2. ServiceManager
 *   3. Services
 *   4. UI or other layers
 * - Provides safe shutdown by stopping services and joining threads.
 *
 * ### Usage
 * ```cpp
 * Core::ServiceManager sm(eventManager);
 * sm.registerService<MyService>(Core::ServiceId::TestService, eventManager);
 * sm.stopAll(); // stops all services and joins threads
 * ```
 *
 * @warning All the services and the CORE are using a common set of enums from the "include_commons" folder. 
 * @warning Copy it inside your project folder, do not modify it directly. All the event types and services ids MUST extend these files !
 *
 * ### Notes
 * - Services must always be created with `std::make_shared`.
 * - Do not stack-allocate services.
 * - `registerService()` calls `init()` and `start()` automatically and launches the service thread.
 * - No service-specific logic is contained in ServiceManager.
 */